      correctIndex: 2,
      explanation: `<div style="--mx-navy:#01506e; --mx-blue:#207bb5; --mx-lightblue:#0099d4; --mx-yellow:#e9dc12; --mx-grey:#4B556A; --mx-rule:#dde4ea; --mx-tint:#f2f7fa; --mx-body:#232a33; color:var(--mx-body); line-height:1.6"><div style="background:var(--mx-tint); border-left:4px solid var(--mx-yellow); padding:13px 18px; margin-bottom:18px"><strong style="color:var(--mx-navy)">C. Plus discount is correct.</strong> The discount is extra borrowing cost paid at maturity, so amortizing it pushes interest expense above the cash actually paid.</div><h3 style="color:var(--mx-navy); font-size:17px; margin:0 0 8px">The rule</h3><p style="margin:0 0 12px">A bond sold at a <strong style="color:var(--mx-navy)">discount</strong> brings in less cash than the issuer must repay at maturity. That shortfall is interest the lender demanded because the stated rate was below the market rate, and it is recognized over the life of the debt as <strong style="color:var(--mx-navy)">discount amortization</strong>. So <strong style="color:var(--mx-navy)">total interest expense = cash interest paid + the discount</strong>.</p><p style="margin:0 0 18px">Reverse the sign for a <strong style="color:var(--mx-navy)">premium</strong>: expense equals cash interest minus the premium. In either case <strong style="color:var(--mx-navy)">par value never enters interest expense</strong>, because repaying principal is a financing outflow, not a cost of borrowing. Amortization also drives the <strong style="color:var(--mx-navy)">carrying value</strong> up to par by the maturity date.</p><h3 style="color:var(--mx-navy); font-size:17px; margin:0 0 8px">The entry each period</h3><p style="margin:0 0 12px">A 3-year, $10,000 bond issued at a $300 discount with $500 annual cash interest, straight-line amortization of $100 per year:</p><div style="background:var(--mx-tint); border-left:4px solid var(--mx-blue); padding:8px 10px; margin-bottom:18px"><table style="border:0; border-collapse:collapse; width:100%"><tbody><tr><td style="border:0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--mx-rule); padding:11px 14px"><strong style="color:var(--mx-navy)">Account</strong></td><td style="border:0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--mx-rule); padding:11px 14px; text-align:right"><strong style="color:var(--mx-navy)">Debit</strong></td><td style="border:0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--mx-rule); padding:11px 14px; text-align:right"><strong style="color:var(--mx-navy)">Credit</strong></td></tr><tr><td style="border:0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--mx-rule); padding:11px 14px">Interest expense</td><td style="border:0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--mx-rule); padding:11px 14px; text-align:right">$600</td><td style="border:0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--mx-rule); padding:11px 14px; text-align:right"></td></tr><tr><td style="border:0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--mx-rule); padding:11px 14px 11px 40px">Discount on bonds payable</td><td style="border:0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--mx-rule); padding:11px 14px; text-align:right"></td><td style="border:0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--mx-rule); padding:11px 14px; text-align:right">$100</td></tr><tr><td style="border:0; padding:11px 14px 11px 40px">Cash</td><td style="border:0; padding:11px 14px; text-align:right"></td><td style="border:0; padding:11px 14px; text-align:right">$500</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h3 style="color:var(--mx-navy); font-size:17px; margin:0 0 8px">Why the others are wrong</h3><p style="margin:0; color:var(--mx-grey)"><strong style="color:var(--mx-navy)">A. Minus discount.</strong> That is the premium treatment, and it understates the true cost of borrowing.<br /><strong style="color:var(--mx-navy)">B. Minus discount minus par value.</strong> Wrong direction on the discount and drags principal repayment into expense.<br /><strong style="color:var(--mx-navy)">D. Plus discount plus par value.</strong> Par is repaid principal, not interest, so it is never added to expense.</p></div>`,
